  "id": "spine-lateral-flexion",
  "name": "Spine Lateral Flexion",
  "definition": "A narrow unilateral bodyweight trunk isolation performed side-lying with the upper foot crossed over the lower leg and externally fixed while the upper body lifts to the directly reviewed 30-degree trunk-elevation endpoint and returns under control. That endpoint is not a segmental lumbar lateral-flexion angle. Equal work is prescribed in both directions because the product's lateral-flexion action and bilateral mesh regions do not encode side. Konrad directly anchors the dynamic fixture, endpoint, external-oblique emphasis, and lumbar-erector and rectus-abdominis coactivation context, but explicitly reports individual freedom in combined hip-joint and segmental-spine motion. Holding the pelvis and hip still is therefore a disclosed catalog coaching adaptation that narrows the product exercise; it is not attributed to the study. Obliques are primary. Quadratus lumborum is secondary through an explicit triangulation of Andersson's condition-matched fine-wire side-lying ipsilateral-flexion evidence, Christophy mechanics, and Konrad's dynamic topology; Konrad did not measure it. The lumbar-extensor aggregate receives stabilizer credit anchored by measured erector-spinae coactivation; multifidus was not separately measured. Rectus abdominis also receives stabilizer credit from measured coactivation. Unfixed legs, standing or kneeling side bends, external loading, side planks, lateral hip lifts, Copenhagen work, rotation, deliberately combined hip motion, and higher-range variants remain outside this contract.",
